\section{What the table shows}{

\strong{Coefficient rows.} One row per fixed-effect coefficient, as for
\code{lm} and \code{glm}. Under \code{exponentiate = TRUE} a \code{glmer} or \code{glmmTMB} fit
follows the link rules of \code{glm}: odds ratios under the logit link, rate
ratios under the log link, an error for a link whose exponential is not
a ratio. The rows are conditional on a random effect of zero: an
exponentiated intercept is the baseline odds or rate of a group whose
random effect is zero, not the average over groups.

\strong{Random-effects rows.} One row per standard deviation and per
correlation of each grouping factor, then the residual of a Gaussian
model, each with its estimate, SE and CI in the coefficient columns. The
rows of a \code{glmer} or \code{glmmTMB} fit are on the scale of the linear
predictor (log-odds, log-rate). The rows carry no p-value: a Wald test
of a variance is invalid at the boundary of its parameter space (Self &
Liang 1987), and \code{re_test} adds a test that is valid there. The note
gives the estimation method (REML or ML) and the likelihood-ratio test
of the whole random part against the model without random effects, with
its boundary-corrected chi-bar-squared p-value (Self & Liang 1987; Stram
& Lee 1994). For an \code{lme} fit the null model keeps the residual
variance and correlation structure of the fit. A \code{gls} fit has no random
effects and no block. \code{glmmTMB} zero-inflation, hurdle and dispersion
components render as further blocks, described in
\link{table_regression_counts} (\code{show_components}).

In \code{broom::tidy()} the variance-component rows have
\code{estimate_type = "vc"}, their terms are prefixed \verb{re::}, and the
correlations carry a \verb{::cor} suffix.

\strong{Fit statistics.} By default, \code{n}, one \verb{N (<group>)} row per grouping
factor (\code{"n_groups"}), the \code{ICC}, the marginal and conditional
R-squared, \code{AIC} and \code{BIC}; see \emph{Fit statistics} below.
}

\section{Arguments for this family}{

These arguments act on the random-effects rows only. They have no
effect on fits without random effects.

\describe{
\item{\code{show_re}}{Logical. \code{TRUE} (default) renders the
\verb{Random effects} block for \code{lmer}, \code{glmer}, \code{glmmTMB}, \code{lme} and
\code{clmm} fits. \code{FALSE} suppresses it. \code{re_test} and
\code{re_ci = "profile"} need the block and are refused with
\code{show_re = FALSE}.}
\item{\code{re_scale}}{\code{"sd"} (default) or \code{"variance"}. \code{"sd"} reports
the standard deviation \eqn{\sigma}{sigma}, in the units of the
linear predictor (those of the outcome for a Gaussian model).
\code{"variance"} reports \eqn{\sigma^2}{sigma^2}, in squared units.
The standard error is a delta-method one on the scale the engine
estimates on, and
\eqn{SE(\sigma) = SE(\sigma^2) / (2\sigma)}{SE(sigma) = SE(sigma^2) / (2 sigma)}.
For \code{lmer} and \code{glmer} it comes from the numerical Hessian of the
deviance (the REML criterion for a REML fit) in the standard
deviations and correlations, i.e. the observed information; for a
\code{glmer} fit spicy uses the Hessian lme4 stores at the optimum when
there is one. The prior weights of an \code{lmer} fit are taken into
account. A negative-binomial \code{glmer}, a \code{glmer} family with a
scale parameter and an lme4 covariance structure other than the
unstructured one keep their rows without SE or CI, and a note under
the table says why. So does a Hessian that is not positive definite.
For \code{lme}, \code{glmmTMB} and \code{clmm} the engine estimates \eqn{\log\sigma}{log(sigma)} and the SE is
\eqn{\sigma \, SE(\log\sigma)}{sigma SE(log(sigma))}. The Wald CI of
every engine is built on \eqn{\log\sigma}{log(sigma)},
\eqn{\sigma \exp(\pm z \, SE(\log\sigma))}{sigma exp(+/- z SE(log(sigma)))},
and squared for \eqn{\sigma^2}{sigma^2}: by spicy for \code{lmer} and
\code{glmer}, by the engine otherwise (\code{nlme::intervals()},
\code{glmmTMB::confint()}). The residual row of a Gaussian \code{glmmTMB} fit
has its SE and CI too. Correlation rows (\eqn{\rho}{rho}) have no unit and are unchanged.
For \code{lmer} and \code{glmer} their SE comes from the same Hessian, and
the CI is the Wald interval on
\eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(\rho)}{atanh(rho)},
\eqn{\tanh(\mathrm{atanh}(\rho) \pm z \, SE / (1 - \rho^2))}{tanh(atanh(rho) +/- z SE / (1 - rho^2))},
which stays inside \eqn{(-1, 1)}{(-1, 1)}. For the other engines
the SE is a delta-method one: from
\eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(\rho)}{atanh(rho)} for \code{lme}, and from glmmTMB's
\eqn{\rho / \sqrt{1 - \rho^2}}{rho / sqrt(1 - rho^2)} in a two-term
block, with the engine's CI. In a \code{glmmTMB} block of three terms or
more it is the width of glmmTMB's interval divided by \eqn{2z}{2z}.
nlme computes the covariance matrix behind the SE of an \code{lme} fit
by finite differences. With nlme's default step and a
random-effect correlation close to zero, the SE can be off by
several percent and differ from one platform to another, or the
matrix is not positive definite and the rows have no SE and no CI,
which a note under the table says.
A larger step, \code{control = nlme::lmeControl(.relStep = 1e-4)},
gives a stable matrix. spicy displays what nlme estimates.}
\item{\code{re_columns}}{Subset of \code{c("est", "se", "ci")}: the cells of the
random-effects rows that are displayed (\code{"est"} is mandatory).
Deselected cells render as an en dash on those rows only:
\code{re_columns = "est"} for estimates alone, \code{c("est", "se")} for a
journal that wants standard errors only. Display only:
\code{broom::tidy()} and \code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}} keep the SE and CI.}
\item{\code{re_test}}{\code{"none"} (default), \code{"lrt"} or \code{"rlrt"}. A per-term
test of the variance components, which fills the p column of the
random-effects rows. Both are likelihood-ratio tests; a Wald test of
a variance is refused (see \emph{What is refused, and why}).
\itemize{
\item \code{"lrt"}: likelihood-ratio test of each random term against
the model refitted without it (its variance and its covariances
with the other terms of its bar), referred to the chi-bar-squared
mixture \verb{0.5 chi2(q-1) + 0.5 chi2(q)} (Self & Liang 1987; Stram
& Lee 1994). The reduction scheme is that of
\code{lmerTest::ranova()}, and the mixture makes the p-value
asymptotically exact instead of conservative. The intercept of a
bar is tested only when it is the only term of the bar.
Available for \code{lmer}, \code{glmer}, \code{glmmTMB}, and \code{lme} with a simple
\code{random = ~ terms | group} structure.
\item \code{"rlrt"}: exact restricted likelihood-ratio test with a
simulated finite-sample null (\code{RLRsim::exactRLRT()}; Crainiceanu
& Ruppert 2004). Defined only for a Gaussian \code{lmer} or \code{lme} fit
with a single variance component; other fits are refused with a
pointer to \code{"lrt"}.
}
The statistic and its df stay out of the t/z column (they are on the
chi-square scale) and are kept in \code{broom::tidy()} (\code{test_type}
\code{"chibar2"} or \code{"rlrt"}). The test of the whole block in the note is
unchanged. Correlation and residual rows are never tested. Each
random term costs one refit; a refit that fails leaves its row
untested, with a \code{spicy_fallback} warning. For other structures,
\code{pbkrtest::PBmodcomp()} gives a parametric-bootstrap test of the
same pair of fits (Halekoh & Højsgaard 2014), to report beside the
table.}
\item{\code{re_ci}}{\code{"wald"} (default) or \code{"profile"}: the uncertainty of
the random-effects rows of \code{lmer} and \code{glmer} fits.
\itemize{
\item \code{"wald"}: SE from the observed information (the Hessian
of the deviance, see \code{re_scale}) and a Wald CI symmetric on the
log of the standard deviation, which stays above 0, and on
\eqn{\mathrm{atanh}(\rho)}{atanh(rho)} for a correlation. Wald
can be optimistic near the boundary of a variance; \code{"profile"}
respects it. The Hessian takes a few dozen evaluations of the
deviance: under a second for an \code{lmer} fit of 20,000
observations. A \code{glmer} fit above 10,000 observations has no
Hessian stored by lme4, and its computation then takes about a
quarter of the time of the fit.
\item \code{"profile"}: profile-likelihood intervals from
\code{confint(fit, method = "profile")}, the route lme4 documents.
They are asymmetric and respect the boundary at 0. No SE is
shown, since a symmetric SE misdescribes the skewed sampling
distribution of a variance. It takes longer than \code{"wald"}
(about two seconds per variance parameter at n of 7,000; more
for \code{glmer}). lme4 profiles the ML deviance, also for a REML
fit: the interval of a REML fit is that of its ML refit, and
the note says so. The note names the method, and
\code{re_scale = "variance"} shows the profile interval of the
variance itself. A negative-binomial \code{glmer}, which has no Wald
SE, gets its profile interval this way. lme4 does not profile a
\code{glmer} whose scale is estimated (Gamma, inverse Gaussian): its
rows keep no interval and a warning says so.
}
\code{glmmTMB}, \code{lme} and \code{clmm} fits keep the intervals of their own
engine under \code{"wald"} (see \code{re_scale}) and refuse \code{"profile"}.}
}

The arguments shared with other classes take these values for mixed
models:
\describe{
\item{\code{vcov}, \code{cluster}}{\code{lmer} and \code{lme}: \code{"classical"} and \code{"CR0"}
to \code{"CR3"}. \code{glmmTMB}: \code{"classical"}, \code{"CR0"} and \code{"CR1"}. \code{glmer}
and \code{gls}: \code{"classical"} only. See \emph{Standard errors, degrees of
freedom and intervals}.}
\item{\code{ci_method}}{\code{"wald"} only. \code{"profile"} is defined for \code{glm} and
the ordinal models, and \code{"boot_percentile"} needs
\code{vcov = "bootstrap"}, which mixed models refuse. The intervals of
the random-effects rows are set by \code{re_ci}.}
\item{\code{standardized}}{\code{"refit"}: the model is refitted on z-scored
data with its random structure, the one approach that is well posed
for a mixed model, since the marginal SD(y) has no unique
decomposition across levels. \code{"posthoc"}, \code{"basic"}, \code{"smart"} and
\code{"pseudo"} fall back to \code{"refit"} with a \code{spicy_fallback} warning.
The \eqn{\beta}{beta} rows take the reference distribution of their B
rows, so B and \eqn{\beta}{beta} carry the same p-value, and a robust
\code{vcov} is applied to the refit too. A formula with an inline
transform (\code{log(x)}, \code{poly()}) cannot be refitted: the
\eqn{\beta}{beta} rows are left out, with a warning; build the
transformed column in \code{data} before fitting. Refused for \code{glmmTMB}
with the ordinal family, whose outcome has no scale.}
\item{\code{show_columns}}{\code{"partial_chi2"} is the Wald chi-square of each
term, Type II by default and Type III under \code{partial_type = "III"}
(the type SAS PROC MIXED and \code{lmerTest} report; the two agree for a
model without interactions). It follows the robust \code{vcov} when one
is set. \code{"partial_f"} and the variance-explained partials
(\code{"partial_f2"}, \code{"partial_eta2"}, \code{"partial_omega2"}) are not
defined. The AME tokens are available for every engine of the table
above except \code{glmmTMB} with the ordinal family or fitted by REML,
whose fixed effects the AME cannot perturb; no column token is
reserved to mixed models.}
\item{\code{show_fit_stats}}{Four tokens belong to this family:
\code{"n_groups"} (also used by GEE and \code{fixest} fits), \code{"icc"},
\code{"r2_marginal"} and \code{"r2_conditional"}. See \emph{Fit statistics}.}
}
}

\section{Standard errors, degrees of freedom and intervals}{

The fixed effects are tested as each engine tests them:
\itemize{
\item A fit of \code{lmerTest::lmer()}: t with Satterthwaite degrees of
freedom.
\item A plain \code{lme4::lmer()} fit: Wald z. A message, once per
session, suggests refitting with \code{lmerTest::lmer()}; the note names
the method only.
\item \code{glmer} and \code{glmmTMB}: Wald z.
\item \code{lme}: t with the containment degrees of freedom of \code{nlme},
named in the note. \code{gls}: t on n minus the number of coefficients.
}
The confidence interval uses the same reference distribution as the
test. The AME rows take the variance matrix of the coefficient rows,
model-based or robust, and are tested by Wald z. Beside the t rows of
an \code{lmerTest} or \code{lme} fit, or of a \verb{CR*} variance, an identity-link
AME can therefore carry another p-value than the B of the same row;
read it as a large-sample approximation.

For a mixed model with a non-identity link, the AME averages the slopes
of the conditional predictions: each observation is predicted with the
estimated random effects of its groups. It is neither the effect for a
typical group (random effects at zero) nor the population-averaged
effect, which integrates over the distribution of the random effects.
In a linear mixed model with random intercepts only, the three are
equal.

Cluster-robust variance. \code{lmer} and \code{lme} fits take \code{"CR0"} to \code{"CR3"}
from \code{\link[clubSandwich:vcovCR]{clubSandwich::vcovCR()}}, with Satterthwaite degrees of freedom
computed from the robust matrix. An \code{lme} fitted with other contrasts
than those of the session is refused: \pkg{clubSandwich} rebuilds its
design with the session contrasts, so its variance would not match the
coefficients. \code{glmmTMB} fits take \code{"CR0"} and \code{"CR1"} from
glmmTMB's own sandwich (\pkg{glmmTMB} >= 1.1.15), which covers the
coefficients, the zero-inflation and dispersion blocks and the AME.
\code{"CR0"} is the sandwich as glmmTMB computes it, with one score per
cluster (the gradient of the marginal log-likelihood of the cluster);
\code{"CR1"} scales it by \eqn{G/(G-1)}{G/(G-1)}, \code{G} the number of
clusters. Both use Wald z. Without \code{cluster}, the cluster is the
top-level grouping factor of the random effects. The estimator needs
independent clusters that contain the random effects, every group
within one cluster, so crossed random effects need a \code{cluster} that
contains them all. It keeps the standard errors valid when the
random-effects distribution or the conditional variance is
misspecified, provided the estimates stay consistent (Liang & Zeger
1986 for a misspecified variance and correlation, Verbeke & Lesaffre
1997 for the random-effects distribution). With few clusters it understates the
variance, which the factor of \code{"CR1"} offsets only in part: this class
has no bias-reduced correction (Cameron & Miller 2015).

The robust estimator covers the fixed effects: their coefficients,
their joint tests (\code{"partial_chi2"}), the standardized coefficients and
the AME. The variance components keep their model-based inference.
glmmTMB's sandwich takes one likelihood score per cluster over the
fixed and variance parameters, while the \verb{CR*} estimators of \code{lmer} and
\code{lme} come from \pkg{clubSandwich} over the fixed effects alone: on the
same model the two agree as the clusters grow, but not exactly.

Hierarchical comparison (\code{nested = TRUE}). The default change rows are
\code{c("aic_change", "bic_change", "lrt_change", "p_change")} (Pinheiro &
Bates 2000, Section 2.4.1), from the engine's own two-model \code{anova()}.
Under \code{"CR0"} to \code{"CR3"}, \code{lmer} and \code{lme} hierarchies use the HTZ
test of \code{clubSandwich::Wald_test()}; under \code{"CR0"} and \code{"CR1"}, a
\code{glmmTMB} hierarchy uses a Wald chi-square. See \emph{Hierarchical (nested)
model comparison} in \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}.
}

\section{Fit statistics}{

By default, \code{c("nobs", "n_groups", "icc", "r2_marginal", "r2_conditional", "aic", "bic")}, for
\code{lmer}, \code{glmer}, \code{glmmTMB}, \code{lme} and \code{clmm} fits. A row of the default that no model of the table fills
is left out. A token named in the call that no model fills is refused.
A
\code{gls} fit gets \code{n} and \code{AIC}.
\itemize{
\item \code{"n_groups"}: one \verb{N (<group>)} row per grouping factor.
\item \code{"icc"}: the intraclass correlation of a model with one random
intercept and no other random term:
\eqn{\sigma^2_u / (\sigma^2_u + \sigma^2_e)}{var_u / (var_u + var_e)}.
For a non-Gaussian \code{glmer} fit the residual variance is the
distribution variance of the link scale (the adjusted ICC of
Nakagawa, Johnson & Schielzeth 2017): \eqn{\pi^2/3}{pi^2/3} for the
logit, 1 for the probit, \eqn{\pi^2/6}{pi^2/6} for the cloglog, and
\eqn{\log(1 + 1/\lambda)}{log(1 + 1/lambda)} for a Poisson log fit.
A binomial \code{cbind()} response, whose rows are not single trials, has
no ICC. For \code{glmmTMB} the ICC is computed for the Gaussian identity
family only. A model with random slopes has no ICC: its cell is
empty. With several
grouping factors there is no single ICC, and the note says so.
\item \code{"r2_marginal"}, \code{"r2_conditional"}: the R-squared of Nakagawa &
Schielzeth (2013), the variance explained by the fixed effects
alone and by the fixed and random effects together; the values
match \code{performance::r2_nakagawa()}. Empty for \code{gls}.
\item On request: \code{"deviance"}, \code{"log_lik"} (the restricted
log-likelihood of a REML fit), \code{"sigma"} (see
\link{table_regression_tokens} for its \code{glmmTMB} families), and the dispersion
rows of a negative-binomial or beta family (\code{"theta"}, \code{"alpha"},
\code{"delta"}, \code{"phi"}).
\item Refused when every model of the table is mixed: \code{"r2"},
\code{"adj_r2"}, \code{"omega2"}, \code{"f2"}, the pseudo-R-squared tokens and
\code{"r2_deviance"}. They have no definition for a mixed model; the
error points to \code{"r2_marginal"} and \code{"r2_conditional"}.
}
}

\section{What is refused, and why}{

\itemize{
\item \verb{HC*}, \code{"bootstrap"} and \code{"jackknife"} for every mixed engine:
these estimators assume independent observations or refit \code{lm} and
\code{glm}, and the random effects exist to model the dependence.
\item \verb{CR*} for \code{glmer} (\pkg{clubSandwich} has no working backend
for it), and \code{"CR2"} and \code{"CR3"} for \code{glmmTMB} (its sandwich has one
score per cluster, with no residuals and no hat values). A robust
\code{vcov} for a \code{glmmTMB} fit by REML or with the ordinal family.
\item A Wald test of a variance component, on any engine: the null
value 0 lies on the boundary of the parameter space.
\item \code{re_ci = "profile"} outside lme4, and \code{re_test = "rlrt"}
outside a Gaussian fit with one variance component.
\item Under \code{nested = TRUE}:
\itemize{
\item A REML pair whose fixed effects differ
(\code{nlme::lme(method = "REML")}, \code{glmmTMB(REML = TRUE)}). The
restricted likelihood depends on the fixed-effects design, so
the likelihood-ratio test is not valid (Pinheiro & Bates 2000,
Section 2.4.2). Refit both by maximum likelihood. A REML pair
that differs only in its random structure stays valid.
\item Fits from different engines. No engine's \code{anova()} accepts
a fit of another; refit one with the other's engine.
\item The variance-explained change tokens (\code{"r2_change"},
\code{"adj_r2_change"}, \code{"f_change"}, \code{"f2_change"}). The comparison
is a chi-square.
}
\code{nested = FALSE} sets the models side by side instead.
}
}

\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("lme4", quietly = TRUE)) {
  fit <- lme4::lmer(Reaction ~ Days + (Days | Subject),
                    data = lme4::sleepstudy)
  print(table_regression(fit))

  # Variances instead of standard deviations, estimates only.
  print(table_regression(fit, re_scale = "variance", re_columns = "est"))

  # Nested comparison, fitted by maximum likelihood.
  m1 <- lme4::lmer(Reaction ~ 1 + (1 | Subject),
                   data = lme4::sleepstudy, REML = FALSE)
  m2 <- lme4::lmer(Reaction ~ Days + (1 | Subject),
                   data = lme4::sleepstudy, REML = FALSE)
  print(table_regression(list(m1, m2), nested = TRUE))
}
}

}
